 "music": "bed",
 "title": "OAuth 2.0",
 "sources": [
  "RFC 6749, The OAuth 2.0 Authorization Framework (fetched 2026-11-17): in the authorization code grant the client directs the resource owner's user-agent to the authorization endpoint, the authorization server redirects the user-agent back to the client with an authorization code, and the client exchanges it at the token endpoint for an access token; the resource owner's credentials are never shared with the client; the authorization server MUST compare and match the received redirection URI against the registered one, using simple string comparison if a full URI was registered. https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc6749",
  "RFC 7636, Proof Key for Code Exchange (fetched 2026-11-17): public clients using the authorization code grant are susceptible to the authorization code interception attack; PKCE uses a per-request code_verifier (43 to 128 characters) and a code_challenge derived from it, S256 = BASE64URL(SHA256(verifier)); the verifier is sent later with the token request; a client able to use S256 MUST use it. https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc7636"
 ],
 "lines": [
  {
   "text": "You click login with Google, and the app never sees your Google password. How does that work?",
   "caps": "*LOGIN WITH GOOGLE | APP NEVER SEES *YOUR PASSWORD"
  },
  {
   "text": "The app sends your browser to Google's authorization server, where you log in and approve.",
   "caps": "BROWSER GOES *TO GOOGLE | YOU *LOG IN | YOU *APPROVE"
  },
  {
   "text": "Your credentials are never shared with the app. Google keeps them.",
   "caps": "CREDENTIALS *NEVER SHARED | *GOOGLE KEEPS THEM"
  },
  {
   "text": "Google then redirects your browser back to the app with an authorization code.",
   "caps": "REDIRECT *BACK | WITH AN *AUTHORIZATION CODE"
  },
  {
   "text": "The app sends that code to the token endpoint, and exchanges it for an access token.",
   "caps": "CODE *EXCHANGED | FOR AN *ACCESS TOKEN"
  },
  {
   "text": "But here's the catch: the code travels through the browser, so an attacker who intercepts it could exchange it too.",
   "caps": "BUT THERE IS A *CATCH | CODE TRAVELS *VIA BROWSER | ATTACKER *INTERCEPTS"
  },
  {
   "text": "PKCE fixes that: the app makes a random secret, sends only its hash first, and must present the original secret later.",
   "caps": "*PKCE | SEND ONLY THE *HASH | PROVE WITH THE *SECRET"
  },
  {
   "text": "And the server must match the redirect URI against the registered one, so only exact matches are allowed.",
   "caps": "REDIRECT URI *MUST MATCH | *EXACT MATCH ONLY"
  }
 ],
